// Thinner subjects, part 9 (28 November 2026): Prehistoric Life, The Modern World, Plants and Ecosystems, Planet Earth, The Human Body.
ADDART({id:"compsognathus",t:"Compsognathus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦎",w:"Compsognathus",r:["archaeopteryx","dinobirds","microraptor","dino","fossils","paleontology"],
p:[["Chicken-sized dinosaur","One of the smallest dinosaurs known."],["Found with its lunch","A lizard was preserved in its stomach."],["Jurassic lagoons","It lived near warm seas in Europe."]],
l:"Compsognathus was a small meat-eating dinosaur that lived about 150 million years ago, in the Late Jurassic Period, on islands in a warm, shallow sea that covered what are now southern Germany and France. It was about 1 meter long, most of that tail, and weighed under 3 kilograms, about the size of a turkey or a large chicken. For more than a century it was famous as the smallest known dinosaur. Compsognathus had a long neck, a small pointed head with sharp teeth, long legs for running and short arms with two main fingers. It was a fast, agile hunter of small animals. Only two good skeletons are known, both preserved in fine limestone: one found in Bavaria, Germany, in the 1850s, which has the skeleton of a lizard inside its ribcage, its last meal; and a larger one found near Nice, France. The same limestone at Solnhofen in Germany also preserved Archaeopteryx. Relatives of Compsognathus from China, such as Sinosauropteryx, are preserved with fuzzy feathers, suggesting Compsognathus may have been feathered too.",
s:[["Discovery","The German skeleton was found in the Solnhofen limestone quarries of Bavaria and named in 1859 by Johann Andreas Wagner. Its name means ‘pretty jaw’. The French specimen, about 25 percent larger, was found in the 1970s and described in 1978."],["The lizard in its stomach","Inside the German skeleton is a small, fast lizard called Bavarisaurus, swallowed whole. It shows that Compsognathus hunted quick prey, and that it swallowed food head-first, as many reptiles and birds do."],["A link to birds","In the 1860s, the English biologist Thomas Henry Huxley pointed out how similar Compsognathus was to Archaeopteryx and to birds, one of the first arguments that birds are related to dinosaurs."],["Feathers or scales?","No feathers are preserved with Compsognathus, but its close relative Sinosauropteryx from China, described in 1996, was the first dinosaur found with feather-like fuzz, which even shows a striped, ginger-colored tail."]],
k:[["Lived","About 150 million years ago (Late Jurassic)"],["Where","Germany and France"],["Length","About 1 meter"],["Weight","Under 3 kilograms"],["Diet","Small animals such as lizards"],["Name means","Pretty jaw"]],
d:[["1859","Compsognathus named from the Bavarian skeleton"],["1868","Huxley compares it with birds"],["1978","French specimen described"],["1996","Feathered relative Sinosauropteryx described"]],
s2:[["Smaller dinosaurs","Several dinosaurs smaller than Compsognathus are now known, such as Microraptor, but it was the smallest known for over 100 years."],["In films","Compsognathus appears in The Lost World: Jurassic Park as a pack of small, biting dinosaurs."]],
g:[["Jurassic","The period from about 201 to 145 million years ago"],["Theropod","A two-legged, mostly meat-eating dinosaur"],["Limestone","Rock made mostly of calcium carbonate"],["Specimen","A single example kept for study"]],
f:["Compsognathus was about the size of a chicken.","A lizard was found in its stomach.","It lived in the Jurassic."],
fun:"The first Compsognathus skeleton still has its last meal, a lizard, inside its ribs."});

ADDART({id:"oviraptor",t:"Oviraptor",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🥚",w:"Oviraptor",r:["dinoeggs","dinobirds","velociraptor","fossils","maiasaura","protoceratops"],
p:[["The ‘egg thief’","It was named for stealing eggs, but it was innocent."],["A caring parent","Relatives were found sitting on their own nests."],["Beaked and feathered","It had a toothless beak and feathered arms."]],
l:"Oviraptor was a small, feathered, beaked dinosaur that lived in what is now the Gobi Desert of Mongolia about 75 million years ago, in the Late Cretaceous. It was about 1.6 to 2 meters long and weighed around 30 kilograms. Its name, meaning ‘egg seizer’, came from the first skeleton, found in 1923 by an American Museum of Natural History expedition lying on top of a nest of eggs. The scientists thought the eggs belonged to the horned dinosaur Protoceratops and that Oviraptor had died raiding the nest. In the 1990s, however, an identical egg was found with an oviraptorid embryo inside, and skeletons of close relatives were found sitting on nests with their arms spread over the eggs, like brooding birds. Oviraptor had been guarding its own eggs. It had a short skull with a strong, toothless beak and a bony crest, long arms with three clawed fingers, and almost certainly feathers. It may have eaten a mix of plants, eggs, shellfish and small animals.",
s:[["Discovery","The first Oviraptor was found at the Flaming Cliffs in Mongolia during the Central Asiatic Expeditions led by Roy Chapman Andrews. The paleontologist Henry Fairfield Osborn named it in 1924, warning that the name might mislead because its diet was uncertain."],["Brooding parents","In 1993 a skeleton of the related Citipati was found sitting on a nest of eggs arranged in a ring, with its arms around them. It had probably been buried by a sandstorm or collapsing dune while protecting the nest."],["Beak","Oviraptor’s strong beak may have been used to crack hard foods such as seeds, nuts or shellfish. Its exact diet is still debated."],["Relatives","Oviraptorosaurs ranged from small animals to the giant Gigantoraptor, about 8 meters long. Some had tall crests like a cassowary’s."]],
k:[["Lived","About 75 million years ago"],["Where","Gobi Desert, Mongolia"],["Length","About 1.6–2 meters"],["Weight","About 30 kilograms"],["Name means","Egg seizer"],["Real story","It was guarding its own eggs"]],
d:[["1923","First skeleton found on a nest"],["1924","Oviraptor named"],["1993","Brooding Citipati found on a nest"],["1994","Embryo shows the eggs were oviraptorid"]],
s2:[["Bird-like","The way oviraptorids sat on nests, laid eggs in pairs and arranged them in rings is very similar to birds today."],["Colored eggs","Studies suggest some oviraptorid eggs were blue-green, like the eggs of emus."]],
g:[["Brooding","Sitting on eggs to keep them warm"],["Embryo","A young animal developing inside an egg"],["Beak","A hard, toothless mouth"],["Oviraptorosaur","A group of beaked, feathered dinosaurs"]],
f:["Oviraptor was wrongly accused of stealing eggs.","Oviraptor had a toothless beak.","It lived in the Gobi Desert."],
fun:"Oviraptor’s name means ‘egg thief’, but it was really a protective parent."});

ADDART({id:"maiasaura",t:"Maiasaura",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪺",w:"Maiasaura",r:["dinoeggs","parasaurolophus","iguanodon","dino","fossils","paleontology","edmontosaurus"],
p:[["Good mother lizard","Its name means ‘good mother lizard’."],["Nesting colonies","Many nests were found close together."],["In space","A Maiasaura bone went to space in 1985."]],
l:"Maiasaura was a large plant-eating duck-billed dinosaur, or hadrosaur, that lived in what is now Montana in the United States about 76 million years ago. It was about 9 meters long and weighed around 3 to 4 tonnes. In 1978 the paleontologists Jack Horner and Bob Makela found a nesting ground with nests, eggs, babies and young dinosaurs at a site later called Egg Mountain. The baby dinosaurs in the nests were too large to be hatchlings but had legs not yet strong enough for walking far, suggesting they stayed in the nest and were fed and protected by their parents. Horner named the dinosaur Maiasaura, meaning ‘good mother lizard’. The discovery showed for the first time that some dinosaurs cared for their young, like birds and crocodiles. Maiasaura nested in large colonies, with nests about 7 meters apart, roughly the length of an adult. A huge bone bed nearby contains the remains of an estimated 10,000 animals, possibly a herd killed by a volcanic eruption or flood.",
s:[["Egg Mountain","At Egg Mountain in western Montana, scientists found nests that were bowl-shaped mounds of earth about 2 meters wide, each with up to about 30 to 40 eggs. Parents may have covered the eggs with rotting plants to keep them warm."],["Growing up fast","Studies of growth rings in Maiasaura bones show babies grew very quickly, from about 40 centimeters at hatching to over 3 meters in their first year, and reached adult size in about eight years."],["Into space","In 1985 a small piece of Maiasaura bone and eggshell was carried to the Spacelab 2 mission aboard the Space Shuttle Challenger, making Maiasaura one of the first dinosaurs in space."],["State fossil","Maiasaura is the state fossil of Montana."]],
k:[["Lived","About 76 million years ago"],["Where","Montana, United States"],["Length","About 9 meters"],["Diet","Plants"],["Group","Hadrosaur (duck-billed dinosaur)"],["Name means","Good mother lizard"]],
d:[["1978","Nesting site found by Horner and Makela"],["1979","Maiasaura named"],["1985","Maiasaura bone carried into space"]],
s2:[["A female name","Maiasaura ends in ‘-saura’, the feminine form, instead of the usual ‘-saurus’, in honor of its motherly behavior."],["Herds","Maiasaura probably traveled in herds, which helped protect them from predators such as tyrannosaurs."]],
g:[["Hadrosaur","A duck-billed plant-eating dinosaur"],["Colony","A group of animals nesting together"],["Hatchling","A newly hatched animal"],["Bone bed","A layer of rock packed with fossil bones"]],
f:["Maiasaura means ‘good mother lizard’.","Maiasaura nested in colonies.","Maiasaura was a duck-billed dinosaur."],
fun:"A tiny piece of Maiasaura bone flew aboard the Space Shuttle in 1985."});

ADDART({id:"dimorphodon",t:"Dimorphodon",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦇",w:"Dimorphodon",r:["pterosaurs","rhamphorhynchus","pteranodon","fossils","ichthyosaurs"],
p:[["Big-headed flyer","An early pterosaur with a huge, puffin-like head."],["Found by Mary Anning","She discovered it on the English coast in 1828."],["Two kinds of teeth","Its name means ‘two-form tooth’."]],
l:"Dimorphodon was an early pterosaur, a flying reptile, that lived about 195 to 190 million years ago in the Early Jurassic, along the coasts of what is now southern England. It had a wingspan of about 1.4 meters, a long tail and a strikingly large, deep head, about 23 centimeters long, with two different kinds of teeth: long fangs at the front of the jaws and many small teeth behind, which gave it its name, meaning ‘two-form tooth’. Its skull was lightened by large openings, so its head was not as heavy as it looks. The first Dimorphodon fossil was found in 1828 by the English fossil collector Mary Anning at Lyme Regis, on the Dorset coast, now part of the Jurassic Coast World Heritage Site. It was one of the first pterosaurs found outside Germany. Scientists once imagined Dimorphodon as a clumsy flyer or a puffin-like seabird, but studies of its legs and jaws suggest it was a capable climber and walker that probably hunted insects and small land animals rather than fish.",
s:[["Mary Anning","Mary Anning, a working-class woman from Lyme Regis, found many of the most important early fossils, including the first correctly identified ichthyosaur skeleton and the first complete plesiosaur. Her Dimorphodon was described by William Buckland in 1829."],["How it lived","Its strong jaw muscles and short, snapping bite suggest it caught fast prey such as insects and small vertebrates. Its feet and claws indicate it may have climbed trees and cliffs."],["Flight","Like all pterosaurs, Dimorphodon flew on wings made of skin stretched from a hugely lengthened fourth finger to its legs. Its long tail may have helped with steering."],["The Jurassic Coast","The cliffs between Exmouth and Studland in southern England expose 185 million years of rocks. Fossils still wash out of them after storms, and collectors find new specimens every year."]],
k:[["Lived","About 195–190 million years ago"],["Where","Southern England"],["Wingspan","About 1.4 meters"],["Head length","About 23 centimeters"],["Discovered","1828, by Mary Anning"],["Name means","Two-form tooth"]],
d:[["1828","Mary Anning finds the first fossil"],["1829","Described by William Buckland"],["1859","Richard Owen names Dimorphodon"],["2001","Jurassic Coast becomes a World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["Not a dinosaur","Pterosaurs were close relatives of dinosaurs but formed a separate group of flying reptiles."],["In films","Dimorphodon appears in Jurassic World, shown swooping at visitors, though the real animal was much smaller than many people imagine."]],
g:[["Pterosaur","A flying reptile from the age of dinosaurs"],["Wingspan","The distance from wingtip to wingtip"],["Jurassic Coast","A fossil-rich coastline in southern England"],["Vertebrate","An animal with a backbone"]],
f:["Dimorphodon was a pterosaur.","Mary Anning found the first Dimorphodon.","Dimorphodon had two kinds of teeth."],
fun:"Dimorphodon’s head was about a third as long as its whole body without the tail."});

ADDART({id:"baryonyx",t:"Baryonyx",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🎣",w:"Baryonyx",r:["spinosaurus","iguanodon","dino","fossils","paleontology","allosaurus"],
p:[["Fish-eating dinosaur","Fish scales were found in its stomach."],["A huge thumb claw","Its name means ‘heavy claw’."],["Found by an amateur","A fossil collector spotted it in a clay pit in 1983."]],
l:"Baryonyx was a large meat-eating dinosaur that lived about 130 to 125 million years ago in what is now southern England. It was about 7.5 to 10 meters long and weighed perhaps 1.2 to 1.7 tonnes. Its name means ‘heavy claw’, after the enormous curved claw, about 31 centimeters long, on each thumb. Baryonyx had a long, narrow, crocodile-like snout with many cone-shaped teeth, ideal for gripping slippery fish, and remains of fish scales were found in its stomach, along with bones of a young Iguanodon, showing it also ate other animals. It was found in 1983 by Bill Walker, an amateur fossil collector, who spotted a large claw in a clay pit in Surrey. Scientists from the Natural History Museum in London then dug up most of the skeleton, one of the most complete meat-eating dinosaurs ever found in Britain. Baryonyx was an early member of the spinosaurids, the family that includes the giant Spinosaurus, and its discovery helped scientists understand those strange fish-eating dinosaurs.",
s:[["Discovery","Bill Walker found the claw in January 1983 at Smokejacks Pit near Ockley in Surrey. The species was named Baryonyx walkeri in his honor in 1986 by Alan Charig and Angela Milner."],["A fishing dinosaur","Its snout, long and narrow like a crocodile’s, had a rosette of teeth at the tip and nostrils set far back, perhaps so it could dip its snout in water. Its big claws might have been used to hook fish, like a grizzly bear."],["Spinosaurids","Baryonyx belongs to the spinosaurids, fish-eating theropods with long snouts. Relatives include Suchomimus from Niger and Irritator from Brazil, as well as Spinosaurus."],["Other finds","Bones possibly belonging to Baryonyx or close relatives have been found in Spain and Portugal. In 2021, two new relatives, Ceratosuchops and Riparovenator, were named from the Isle of Wight."]],
k:[["Lived","About 130–125 million years ago"],["Where","Southern England"],["Length","About 7.5–10 meters"],["Thumb claw","About 31 cm"],["Diet","Fish and other animals"],["Group","Spinosaurid theropod"]],
d:[["1983","Bill Walker finds the claw in Surrey"],["1986","Baryonyx walkeri named"],["1997","Baryonyx skeleton featured in a new dinosaur gallery in London"],["2021","Two spinosaurid relatives named from the Isle of Wight"]],
s2:[["On display","The Baryonyx skeleton is kept at the Natural History Museum in London, where visitors can see a cast of it."],["Claw first","The giant claw was the first part found, which is why the dinosaur was named after it."]],
g:[["Spinosaurid","A family of fish-eating theropods"],["Snout","The long front part of the face"],["Amateur","Someone who does something as a hobby"],["Theropod","A two-legged, mostly meat-eating dinosaur"]],
f:["Baryonyx ate fish.","Baryonyx had a huge thumb claw.","An amateur collector discovered Baryonyx."],
fun:"Baryonyx’s thumb claw was about as long as a school ruler."});

ADDART({id:"deinosuchus",t:"Deinosuchus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐊",w:"Deinosuchus",r:["croc","trex","massext","fossils","paleontology","titanoboa","sarcosuchus"],
p:[["A giant alligator relative","Up to about 10 meters long."],["Dinosaur hunter","Bite marks show it attacked dinosaurs."],["Banana-sized teeth","Its big rear teeth crushed bones and shells."]],
l:"Deinosuchus was a gigantic relative of alligators that lived along the coasts, swamps and rivers of North America about 82 to 73 million years ago, in the Late Cretaceous, when a shallow sea divided the continent into two. It is one of the largest crocodile-like animals ever known, reaching about 8 to 10 meters long and weighing several tonnes. Its name means ‘terrible crocodile’. Deinosuchus had a broad, rounded snout and huge teeth; those at the back of its jaws were thick and blunt, perfect for crushing turtle shells and bones. Dinosaur bones from Texas and Montana carry bite marks matching Deinosuchus teeth, showing that it attacked or scavenged dinosaurs, including large duck-billed hadrosaurs, perhaps ambushing them at the water’s edge as crocodiles do with wildebeest today. Its bite force has been estimated at many times that of a living saltwater crocodile. Studies published in 2020 suggested its bulbous snout and skull were more alligator-like than once thought, and that it was a close relative of the alligator family.",
s:[["A divided continent","During the Late Cretaceous, the Western Interior Seaway split North America into Laramidia in the west and Appalachia in the east. Deinosuchus fossils are found on both sides, from Montana and Texas to Georgia and New Jersey."],["Bite marks","Tooth marks on hadrosaur and tyrannosaur bones show that Deinosuchus fed on dinosaurs. One study found Deinosuchus bite marks on the vertebrae of a large tyrannosaur relative."],["Size debate","Estimates vary because no complete skeleton has been found. Most recent studies put the largest individuals at about 8 to 10 meters, far bigger than any living crocodile."],["Holes in the snout","Deinosuchus had two large, mysterious holes near the tip of its snout. Their purpose is unknown."]],
k:[["Lived","About 82–73 million years ago"],["Where","North America"],["Length","About 8–10 meters"],["Diet","Turtles, fish, dinosaurs"],["Relatives","Alligators"],["Name means","Terrible crocodile"]],
d:[["1858","First teeth found in North Carolina"],["1909","Deinosuchus named"],["2002","Bite marks on dinosaur bones described"],["2020","Study redescribes its skull"]],
s2:[["Living giants","The largest living crocodile, the saltwater crocodile, reaches about 6 to 7 meters, while Deinosuchus was up to around 10 meters."],["Long life","Like modern crocodilians, Deinosuchus probably kept growing throughout its life, which may have lasted 50 years or more."]],
g:[["Crocodilian","Crocodiles, alligators, caimans and gharials"],["Western Interior Seaway","A sea that once split North America"],["Bite force","How hard an animal can bite"],["Ambush","A surprise attack from hiding"]],
f:["Deinosuchus was a relative of alligators.","Deinosuchus attacked dinosaurs.","Deinosuchus lived in North America."],
fun:"Deinosuchus was so big that it could hunt dinosaurs as large as a school bus."});

ADDART({id:"wildfires",t:"Wildfires",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🔥",w:"Wildfire",r:["climchange","extremeheat","droughts","firefighters","airpollution","deforestation"],
p:[["Fires in nature","Unplanned fires in forests, grasslands and bush."],["Worse in a warming world","Heat and drought are making many fire seasons longer."],["Record 2026","Early 2026 saw a record burned area worldwide."]],
l:"A wildfire is an unplanned fire that burns in forests, grasslands, shrublands or peatlands. Fires need three things, heat, fuel and oxygen, often called the fire triangle. Wildfires can be started by lightning or by people, through campfires, discarded cigarettes, sparks from power lines or machinery, or deliberate arson. Hot, dry and windy weather helps them spread quickly. Fire is a natural part of many ecosystems: some pine cones only open to release seeds after a fire, and regular fires clear dead plants and help grasslands renew. But wildfires can also destroy homes and lives, kill wildlife and fill the air with smoke that harms health far away. Climate change is making hot, dry ‘fire weather’ more common in many regions, lengthening fire seasons. Recent years have seen record fires in Canada (2023), Australia (2019–20), the Mediterranean, the Amazon and Siberia, and devastating fires around Los Angeles in January 2025. In the first four months of 2026, a record of more than 150 million hectares burned worldwide, mostly in Africa and Asia.",
s:[["How wildfires spread","Fires spread faster uphill, in strong winds and through dry fuel. Burning embers can be blown more than a kilometer ahead, starting new spot fires. The largest fires create their own weather, including pyrocumulonimbus clouds that can produce lightning."],["Fighting fires","Firefighters clear firebreaks, strips of land without fuel, drop water and fire retardant from aircraft, and set controlled backburns to remove fuel in front of a fire. Satellites help spot and track fires."],["Living with fire","Many Indigenous peoples, such as Aboriginal Australians, have long used small, cool ‘cultural burns’ to manage land. Today, many countries use planned burns, clear vegetation around homes and build with fire-resistant materials."],["Smoke and health","Wildfire smoke contains tiny particles that can travel thousands of kilometers. Smoke from Canada’s 2023 fires turned skies orange in New York and reached Europe. It can worsen asthma and heart and lung diseases."]],
k:[["Fire triangle","Heat, fuel and oxygen"],["Causes","Lightning and human activity"],["Worsened by","Heat, drought and wind"],["Record season","Canada, 2023"],["Early 2026","Over 150 million hectares burned (January–April)"],["Natural role","Renewing some ecosystems"]],
d:[["1910","Great Fire of 1910 in the northwestern United States"],["2019–20","Australia’s ‘Black Summer’ fires"],["2023","Canada’s worst fire season on record"],["2025","Los Angeles wildfires"],["2026","Record burned area worldwide in January–April"]],
s2:[["Fire-loving plants","Some eucalyptus trees and banksias sprout new shoots from their trunks after fire, and some seeds need smoke to germinate."],["Zombie fires","Fires can smolder underground in peat through the winter and flare up again in spring, sometimes called zombie fires."]],
g:[["Wildfire","An unplanned fire in natural vegetation"],["Firebreak","A strip of land cleared to stop fire"],["Backburn","A controlled fire set to remove fuel"],["Fire weather","Hot, dry, windy conditions that help fires spread"]],
f:["Wildfires need heat, fuel and oxygen.","Climate change is making fire weather more common.","Some plants need fire to release seeds."],
fun:"Some pine cones stay tightly sealed for years and open only in the heat of a fire."});

ADDART({id:"landmines",t:"Landmines",c:"Economics and Society",i:"⚠️",w:"Land mine",r:["redcross","un","wwi","ww2","coldwar","migration"],
p:[["Hidden weapons","Mines stay buried and dangerous long after wars end."],["Mostly harm civilians","Nine in ten casualties are civilians."],["A ban treaty","The 1997 Mine Ban Treaty outlaws antipersonnel mines."]],
l:"A landmine is an explosive device hidden in or on the ground that blows up when someone steps on it or a vehicle drives over it. Antipersonnel mines target people; antitank mines target vehicles. Mines can stay active for decades after a war ends, killing and maiming farmers, children and returning refugees, and making land unusable for farming, roads and homes. Together with other unexploded remains of war, they killed or injured 6,279 people in 2024, the highest yearly total since 2020; about 90 percent were civilians, and nearly half of those were children. In 1997, after a campaign by the International Campaign to Ban Landmines, which won the Nobel Peace Prize that year, and supported by public figures including Diana, Princess of Wales, countries agreed the Mine Ban Treaty, also called the Ottawa Treaty, which bans the use, production and stockpiling of antipersonnel mines. By 2025, 166 countries had joined, but major military powers including the United States, Russia and China had not, and in 2025 Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, Finland and Poland announced they would withdraw, citing security concerns.",
s:[["Clearing mines","Deminers search land slowly and carefully, using metal detectors, specially trained dogs and even African giant pouched rats, which can sniff out explosives and are too light to set them off. Machines with flails and rollers also clear large areas."],["Most affected","In 2024, Myanmar recorded the most casualties for the second year in a row, followed by countries including Syria, Afghanistan and Ukraine. Cambodia, Angola, Bosnia and Herzegovina and Laos still have large areas contaminated from past wars."],["Helping survivors","Survivors often need artificial limbs, rehabilitation, counseling and help to earn a living. The Mine Ban Treaty requires countries to assist victims."],["Success stories","More than 30 countries and areas have been declared free of known mined areas since the treaty, including Mozambique in 2015, once one of the most heavily mined countries."]],
k:[["Types","Antipersonnel and antitank"],["Casualties in 2024","6,279 (with other explosive remnants)"],["Civilian share","About 90 percent"],["Ban treaty","Mine Ban (Ottawa) Treaty, 1997"],["States Parties (2025)","166"],["Nobel Peace Prize","International Campaign to Ban Landmines, 1997"]],
d:[["1992","International Campaign to Ban Landmines formed"],["1997","Mine Ban Treaty signed in Ottawa"],["1999","Treaty enters into force"],["2015","Mozambique declared free of known minefields"],["2025","Five European countries announce withdrawal"]],
s2:[["Hero rats","A rat named Magawa found more than 100 landmines and other explosives in Cambodia and was awarded a gold medal for bravery in 2020."],["International Mine Awareness Day","The United Nations marks 4 April as the International Day for Mine Awareness and Assistance in Mine Action."]],
g:[["Landmine","A hidden explosive set off by pressure or contact"],["Antipersonnel","Designed to harm people"],["Demining","Finding and removing mines"],["Explosive remnants of war","Unexploded bombs, shells and mines left after fighting"]],
f:["Most landmine casualties are civilians.","The Mine Ban Treaty was agreed in 1997.","Trained rats help find landmines."],
fun:"Some deminers are giant pouched rats, which can check an area the size of a tennis court in about 30 minutes."});

ADDART({id:"precisionmedicine",t:"Precision Medicine",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🧬",w:"Precision medicine",r:["genome","dna","crispr","genetherapy","aimedicine","stemcells"],
p:[["Treatment for you","Matching care to a person’s genes, body and life."],["Targeted cancer drugs","Some drugs attack specific gene changes."],["Big gene databases","Millions of volunteers share DNA for research."]],
l:"Precision medicine, also called personalized medicine, is an approach to health care that tailors prevention and treatment to individual people, based on their genes, environment and lifestyle, rather than using one approach for everyone. It became possible largely because of the Human Genome Project, completed in 2003, and because sequencing a person’s DNA has become far cheaper and faster. Doctors can now test tumors to find the specific gene changes driving a cancer and choose drugs that target them, such as imatinib for a type of leukemia or treatments for lung cancers with particular mutations. Genetic tests can show whether a person is likely to respond to a medicine or suffer side effects, an area called pharmacogenomics. Large research projects, such as the UK Biobank, the All of Us program in the United States and similar projects in many countries, collect DNA and health data from hundreds of thousands to millions of volunteers to find links between genes and disease. Precision medicine also raises questions about cost, privacy and fair access.",
s:[["Targeted cancer treatment","Imatinib (Gleevec), approved in 2001, blocks the faulty protein made by a gene fusion in chronic myeloid leukemia and turned a deadly disease into a manageable one for many patients. Many cancers are now tested for gene changes to guide treatment."],["Pharmacogenomics","Genes affect how the body processes medicines. For example, testing for certain gene variants before prescribing some drugs can prevent dangerous reactions."],["Rare diseases","Sequencing a whole genome can diagnose rare genetic diseases in newborns and children, sometimes in just days, allowing faster treatment."],["Concerns","Genetic data is highly personal, so protecting privacy is important. Most genetic research has studied people of European descent, so results may work less well for others, and many projects now aim to include more diverse volunteers."]],
k:[["Also called","Personalized medicine"],["Based on","Genes, environment and lifestyle"],["Made possible by","Cheap DNA sequencing, Human Genome Project"],["Example","Targeted cancer drugs"],["Gene–drug field","Pharmacogenomics"],["Big projects","UK Biobank, All of Us"]],
d:[["2001","Imatinib approved for leukemia"],["2003","Human Genome Project completed"],["2006","UK Biobank begins recruiting"],["2015","US Precision Medicine Initiative announced"],["2018","All of Us program opens enrollment"]],
s2:[["The $1,000 genome","Sequencing the first human genome cost about 3 billion US dollars; by the 2020s, a whole genome could be sequenced for a few hundred dollars."],["Biomarkers","A biomarker is a measurable sign, such as a gene change or protein, that helps doctors predict or track disease."]],
g:[["Precision medicine","Care tailored to an individual"],["Genome","All of an organism’s DNA"],["Mutation","A change in DNA"],["Pharmacogenomics","How genes affect responses to medicines"]],
f:["Precision medicine tailors treatment to individuals.","Some cancer drugs target specific gene changes.","Genes affect how people respond to medicines."],
fun:"Sequencing the first human genome took 13 years; today a genome can be read in about a day."});

ADDART({id:"fastfood",t:"Fast Food",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🍔",w:"Fast food",r:["trade","obesity","nutri","fastfashion","labor"],
p:[["Quick, cheap meals","Food prepared and served in minutes."],["Global chains","Brands sell the same food around the world."],["Health debates","Often high in salt, sugar and fat."]],
l:"Fast food is food that is prepared and served quickly, usually at restaurants with counter or drive-through service and a limited menu, such as burgers, fried chicken, pizza, sandwiches and fries. Quick food is ancient: ancient Rome had street counters selling hot food, and many cultures have long traditions of street food. Modern fast-food chains began in the United States in the early 20th century, with White Castle hamburgers in 1921, and grew rapidly after the 1950s, when Ray Kroc expanded McDonald’s using a system of standardized food, assembly-line kitchens and franchising, in which local owners pay to run restaurants under a brand. Today, giant chains such as McDonald’s, KFC, Subway, Burger King, Domino’s and Starbucks operate in more than 100 countries, adapting menus to local tastes, while chains from other countries, such as Jollibee and Pollo Campero, have expanded abroad. Fast food is popular for being cheap, convenient and consistent, but many items are high in calories, salt, sugar and fat, and diets heavy in fast food are linked to obesity and other health problems.",
s:[["McDonald’s","Brothers Richard and Maurice McDonald opened a fast, self-service burger restaurant in San Bernardino, California, in 1948. Ray Kroc began franchising in 1955 and later bought the company. By the mid-2020s McDonald’s had more than 40,000 restaurants and aimed for 50,000 by the end of 2027."],["Local twists","Fast-food chains often adapt to local food cultures: McDonald’s in India serves no beef and offers vegetarian burgers; Japanese chains sell teriyaki and rice burgers; and many countries have their own home-grown chains."],["Health","Many governments require chains to show calories on menus, and some tax sugary drinks. Chains have added salads, fruit and smaller portions, though critics say marketing to children remains a concern."],["Work","Fast-food restaurants are a common first job for young people. Workers in several countries have campaigned for higher wages, such as the ‘Fight for $15’ movement in the United States from 2012."]],
k:[["Typical foods","Burgers, fried chicken, pizza, fries, sandwiches"],["First burger chain","White Castle, 1921"],["Business model","Franchising"],["Largest burger chain","McDonald’s (over 40,000 restaurants)"],["Health concerns","High salt, sugar, fat and calories"]],
d:[["1921","White Castle opens"],["1948","McDonald brothers open their fast restaurant"],["1952","Kentucky Fried Chicken franchising begins"],["1955","Ray Kroc opens his first McDonald’s franchise"],["1990","McDonald’s opens in Moscow"]],
s2:[["Slow food","The Slow Food movement began in Italy in 1986, after protests over a McDonald’s near Rome’s Spanish Steps, to promote local, traditional cooking."],["Drive-throughs","The first drive-through restaurants appeared in the United States in the 1940s; today many fast-food sales come through drive-through lanes and delivery apps."]],
g:[["Fast food","Quickly prepared and served food"],["Franchise","A license to run a business under a brand"],["Drive-through","Service through a window to people in cars"],["Calorie","A unit of food energy"]],
f:["Fast-food chains use franchising.","White Castle was the first burger chain.","Fast food is often high in salt, sugar and fat."],
fun:"McDonald’s opening in Moscow in 1990 drew about 30,000 customers on its first day."});

ADDART({id:"sneakerculture",t:"Sneaker Culture",c:"Economics and Society",i:"👟",w:"Sneaker collecting",r:["clothing","fastfashion","hiphop","bball","influencers","fandom"],
p:[["More than shoes","Sneakers became symbols of style and identity."],["Basketball and hip hop","Athletes and musicians made them famous."],["Collecting and reselling","Rare pairs sell for huge prices."]],
l:"Sneaker culture is the collecting, trading and celebration of athletic shoes as fashion and personal expression. Rubber-soled canvas shoes appeared in the late 1800s, and the Converse All Star, introduced in 1917 and later named for basketball player Chuck Taylor, became one of the best-selling shoes ever. Sneakers moved from sports into everyday fashion from the 1970s and 1980s, helped by basketball, running booms, skateboarding and hip hop. In 1985 Nike released the Air Jordan, designed for basketball star Michael Jordan, and in 1986 the hip-hop group Run-DMC released the song ‘My Adidas’, leading to one of the first big deals between a music group and a sportswear company. Today, ‘sneakerheads’ around the world line up for limited releases, enter online raffles, and buy and sell rare pairs on resale websites, where some sell for many times their original price. Collaborations with designers, artists and musicians have made sneakers a major part of the global fashion industry. Critics point to waste, labor conditions in factories and the environmental cost of producing billions of shoes a year.",
s:[["Famous shoes","Classic designs include the Converse Chuck Taylor All Star, adidas Superstar and Stan Smith, Puma Suede, Vans Old Skool, Nike Air Force 1 and Air Jordan 1. Many have been re-released many times."],["Resale market","Limited editions can sell out in minutes. Resale platforms let collectors trade them, with prices set by demand. Game-worn or historic sneakers have sold at auction for over a million US dollars."],["Around the world","Sneaker culture thrives in cities from New York and London to Tokyo, Seoul, Lagos and Manila, with conventions such as Sneaker Con drawing thousands of fans."],["Sustainability","Most sneakers are made from plastics and rubber that are hard to recycle. Some brands now use recycled materials, offer repairs or take back old shoes."]],
k:[["Also called","Trainers, kicks, running shoes"],["Famous early shoe","Converse All Star (1917)"],["Air Jordan launched","1985"],["Fans called","Sneakerheads"],["Big influences","Basketball, hip hop, skateboarding"]],
d:[["1917","Converse All Star introduced"],["1949","Adolf Dassler founds adidas"],["1985","Nike Air Jordan released"],["1986","Run-DMC’s ‘My Adidas’"],["2010s","Online resale platforms grow"]],
s2:[["Sneakers or trainers?","Americans usually say sneakers, because the rubber soles made them quiet; in Britain they are often called trainers."],["Museum pieces","Museums such as the Bata Shoe Museum in Toronto have held exhibitions on sneaker history and design."]],
g:[["Sneakerhead","A sneaker collector or enthusiast"],["Limited release","A product made in small numbers"],["Resale","Selling something again, often for more"],["Collaboration","A product designed by a brand with an artist or designer"]],
f:["The Air Jordan was released in 1985.","Sneaker collectors are called sneakerheads.","Hip hop helped make sneakers fashionable."],
fun:"Sneakers got their name because their rubber soles let people ‘sneak’ around quietly."});

ADDART({id:"creatoreconomy",t:"The Creator Economy",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🎥",w:"Creator economy",r:["youtube","influencers","shortvideo","livestreaming","podcasts","gigeconomy"],
p:[["Making a living online","People earn money by making videos, posts and podcasts."],["Many ways to earn","Ads, sponsorships, subscriptions and merchandise."],["Few big earners","Most creators earn little; a few earn a lot."]],
l:"The creator economy is the system of businesses and people who earn money by making content, such as videos, music, art, writing, podcasts and livestreams, and sharing it online. Platforms such as YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, Twitch, Patreon and Substack let anyone with a phone or computer reach a global audience. Creators earn money in several ways: a share of advertising revenue shown alongside their content, sponsorship deals with brands, fan subscriptions and donations, selling merchandise or digital products, and payments from platforms’ creator funds. YouTube began sharing ad revenue with video makers in 2007, helping to start the industry; today, the most popular creators have audiences of hundreds of millions and run large companies. However, studies consistently find that most creators earn little or nothing, and only a small fraction make a full-time living. Creators also face pressures such as burnout, online harassment and sudden changes to platform rules or algorithms that can cut their income. Many countries have introduced rules requiring creators to label paid promotions.",
s:[["Ways to earn","Advertising pays creators based on views. Sponsorships, where brands pay creators to feature products, are often the biggest source of income. Fans can also pay directly through memberships, tips or crowdfunding."],["Algorithms","Platforms use algorithms to decide which content to recommend. Creators often study and adapt to these systems, and changes can make their views rise or fall sharply."],["Kid creators","Some of the most-watched channels feature children. Several places, including France and some US states, have passed laws to protect child influencers’ earnings and privacy."],["Labeling ads","Rules in many countries require creators to clearly disclose when they are paid to promote a product, for example with labels such as ‘ad’ or ‘paid partnership’."]],
k:[["Platforms","YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, Twitch, Patreon, Substack"],["Income sources","Ads, sponsorships, subscriptions, merchandise"],["YouTube ad sharing began","2007"],["Challenges","Low pay for most, burnout, algorithm changes"],["Rules","Paid promotions must be labeled"]],
d:[["2005","YouTube founded"],["2007","YouTube Partner Program begins"],["2011","Twitch launches"],["2013","Patreon founded"],["2023","France passes a law on influencers"]],
s2:[["Not just video","Writers publish paid newsletters, musicians sell directly to fans, and artists sell digital designs, all part of the creator economy."],["Studios at home","Many creators film in bedrooms or home studios, using phones, ring lights and free editing software."]],
g:[["Creator","A person who makes content to share online"],["Sponsorship","Payment from a brand to promote it"],["Algorithm","Rules a computer follows to recommend content"],["Monetization","Turning content into income"]],
f:["Creators can earn from ads, sponsors and fans.","Most creators earn little money.","Paid promotions must be labeled in many countries."],
fun:"The first video ever uploaded to YouTube, in 2005, was a 19-second clip at a zoo."});

ADDART({id:"corkoak",t:"The Cork Oak",c:"Plants",i:"🍾",w:"Quercus suber",r:["oaks","trees","med","portugalcountry","conservation"],
p:[["Bark that regrows","Its thick bark can be stripped without killing the tree."],["Wine corks and more","Cork is used for stoppers, floors and insulation."],["Mediterranean forests","Portugal is the world’s largest cork producer."]],
l:"The cork oak (Quercus suber) is an evergreen oak tree native to the western Mediterranean, especially Portugal, Spain, Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia, Italy and France. Its outer bark is a thick, spongy layer of cork, which protects the tree from fire and drought. Remarkably, this bark can be carefully stripped off by hand without harming the tree, and it grows back. The first harvest happens when a tree is about 25 years old, and after that the bark can be harvested every nine years for the tree’s life of around 200 years. Cork is light, waterproof, elastic and a good insulator, because it is made of millions of tiny air-filled cells. It is famous as stoppers for wine bottles, but is also used for flooring, shoes, insulation, sports equipment and even heat shields on spacecraft. Portugal produces about half of the world’s cork. Cork oak woodlands, called montado in Portugal and dehesa in Spain, are rich habitats, home to endangered animals such as the Iberian lynx and the Spanish imperial eagle.",
s:[["Harvesting cork","Skilled workers use axes to cut and peel large sheets of bark in early summer, when it comes away most easily. The number of the harvest year is painted on the trunk so harvesters know when it can be stripped again."],["Why cork works","In 1665 the English scientist Robert Hooke looked at a thin slice of cork under a microscope and saw tiny boxes he called ‘cells’, the first use of the word in biology. These air-filled cells make cork light and springy."],["A rich habitat","Cork oak woodlands mix scattered trees with grazing land. They support many birds, the Iberian lynx and the Barbary deer, and help prevent soil erosion and desertification."],["Corks versus caps","Many winemakers switched to plastic stoppers and screw caps from the 1990s, but cork producers point out that natural cork is renewable and recyclable and supports forests and jobs."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Quercus suber"],["Native to","Western Mediterranean"],["First harvest","At about 25 years old"],["Harvested every","About 9 years"],["Lifespan","About 200 years"],["Biggest producer","Portugal (about half of world cork)"]],
d:[["1665","Robert Hooke sees cork cells"],["2011","Cork oak declared Portugal’s national tree"]],
s2:[["The Whistler Tree","The Whistler Tree in Portugal, planted in 1783, is one of the largest cork oaks; one harvest produced enough cork for about 100,000 bottle stoppers."],["Space travel","Cork-based materials have been used in heat shields to protect spacecraft from high temperatures."]],
g:[["Cork","The light, spongy outer bark of the cork oak"],["Evergreen","Keeping leaves all year"],["Montado","Portuguese cork oak woodland"],["Insulator","A material that slows heat flow"]],
f:["Cork comes from the bark of the cork oak.","Cork bark grows back after harvesting.","Portugal produces the most cork."],
fun:"The word ‘cell’ in biology comes from Robert Hooke looking at cork under a microscope."});

ADDART({id:"plantevolution",t:"How Plants Conquered Land",c:"Plants",i:"🌿",w:"Evolutionary history of plants",r:["algae","ferns","conifers","flower","photo","fossils"],
p:[["From the water","Plants evolved from green algae."],["Step by step","Mosses, then ferns, then seeds, then flowers."],["Changing the planet","Land plants transformed the air and soil."]],
l:"Plants first moved from water onto land roughly 470 to 500 million years ago, evolving from freshwater green algae. Life on land was a huge challenge: plants had to avoid drying out, support themselves without water to hold them up, and reproduce without swimming sperm. The earliest land plants were small and simple, like today’s liverworts and mosses, without roots or veins. Over millions of years, plants developed new features: a waxy coating to hold in water, tiny pores called stomata to breathe, and around 420 million years ago, veins that carried water upward, allowing taller plants such as ferns and horsetails. By about 385 million years ago the first forests had appeared. Seeds, which protect and feed a young plant, evolved about 360 million years ago, letting plants spread into drier places; conifers became widespread in the age of dinosaurs. Flowering plants appeared around 130 million years ago or a little earlier and spread rapidly, and today they make up most plant species. Land plants changed the planet, building soils, adding oxygen and providing food and homes for animals.",
s:[["Key inventions","A waxy cuticle stopped water loss. Stomata let carbon dioxide in. Vascular tissue, xylem and phloem, carried water and food. Roots anchored plants and found water. Seeds and later flowers and fruits helped plants reproduce and spread."],["The first forests","Fossil tree stumps at Cairo, New York, about 385 million years old, are among the oldest known forests. Trees called Archaeopteris had wood like conifers but spread by spores."],["Coal forests","In the Carboniferous Period, about 359 to 299 million years ago, swampy forests of giant club mosses and tree ferns grew so thickly that their buried remains formed much of the world’s coal."],["Flowers","Flowering plants, or angiosperms, spread quickly in the Cretaceous, alongside insects that pollinated them. Charles Darwin called their sudden rise in the fossil record an ‘abominable mystery’."]],
k:[["Ancestors","Freshwater green algae"],["First land plants","About 470–500 million years ago"],["Veins appear","About 420 million years ago"],["First forests","About 385 million years ago"],["First seeds","About 360 million years ago"],["Flowering plants","About 130 million years ago or earlier"]],
d:[["c. 470 Ma","Earliest land plant spores"],["c. 420 Ma","Cooksonia, an early vascular plant"],["c. 385 Ma","First forests"],["c. 360 Ma","First seed plants"],["c. 130 Ma","Flowering plants spread"]],
s2:[["Fungal partners","Early land plants teamed up with fungi, which helped them absorb water and nutrients from rock and soil, a partnership that continues today in mycorrhizal fungi."],["Changing the air","The spread of forests drew carbon dioxide from the air and may have helped cool Earth’s climate in the Devonian and Carboniferous."]],
g:[["Vascular plant","A plant with veins to carry water and food"],["Stomata","Tiny pores on leaves"],["Spore","A tiny cell that grows into a new plant"],["Angiosperm","A flowering plant"]],
f:["Land plants evolved from green algae.","Seeds evolved before flowers.","The first forests grew about 385 million years ago."],
fun:"For millions of years, the tallest things on land were giant fungi, not trees."});

ADDART({id:"wildflowers",t:"Wildflowers and Meadows",c:"Plants",i:"🌼",w:"Meadow",r:["flower","bee","butterfly","biodivloss","dandelions","grasslands"],
p:[["Flowers that grow on their own","Wildflowers grow without being planted."],["Meadows","Grassy fields full of flowers and insects."],["Vanishing","Many meadows have been lost to farming and building."]],
l:"Wildflowers are flowering plants that grow naturally in the wild, without being planted or cared for by people. They include poppies, daisies, buttercups, clovers, cornflowers, lupines, orchids and many thousands of others found in meadows, prairies, woodlands, mountains, deserts and roadsides around the world. A meadow is an open field of grasses and wildflowers. Some meadows are natural, such as alpine meadows above the tree line; others were created over centuries by people who cut them for hay each summer, which stopped bushes and trees from taking over. Meadows are among the richest habitats for insects, supporting bees, butterflies, beetles and other pollinators, as well as birds and small mammals. Many have disappeared: the United Kingdom, for example, lost about 97 percent of its wildflower meadows in the 20th century as farming changed. Today, people restore meadows, plant wildflower strips along farms and roads, and let lawns grow wild to help pollinators. Some places, such as the deserts of California and Chile and the Namaqualand region of South Africa, are famous for spectacular ‘superblooms’ after rare heavy rains.",
s:[["Superblooms","In deserts, wildflower seeds can lie dormant in the soil for years. After unusually wet winters, they sprout all at once, carpeting hills in color. The Atacama Desert in Chile and Anza-Borrego in California have had famous superblooms."],["Hay meadows","Traditional hay meadows are cut once a year after flowers have set seed. Because the hay is removed, the soil stays low in nutrients, which stops tall grasses from crowding out flowers."],["Prairies","The North American prairie was once a vast grassland full of wildflowers such as coneflowers and blazing stars. Less than 1 percent of the original tallgrass prairie remains."],["Helping pollinators","Planting native wildflowers, mowing less often and avoiding pesticides helps bees and butterflies. Some cities and countries have campaigns such as ‘No Mow May’."]],
k:[["Wildflowers","Flowering plants growing naturally"],["Meadow","An open field of grasses and flowers"],["Hay meadow","Cut once a year for hay"],["UK meadows lost","About 97% in the 20th century"],["Superbloom regions","Atacama, California, Namaqualand"]],
s2:[["State and national flowers","Many countries and regions choose wildflowers as symbols, such as the California poppy and the edelweiss."],["Seed banks in soil","A single square meter of meadow soil can hold thousands of dormant seeds waiting for the right conditions."]],
g:[["Wildflower","A flower that grows without being planted"],["Meadow","A field of grasses and wildflowers"],["Pollinator","An animal that carries pollen between flowers"],["Superbloom","A burst of desert wildflowers after rain"]],
f:["Wildflowers grow without being planted.","Meadows support many pollinators.","Superblooms happen after heavy rain in deserts."],
fun:"Some desert wildflower seeds can wait in the ground for decades for a rainy year."});

ADDART({id:"woodtimber",t:"Wood and Timber",c:"Plants",i:"🪵",w:"Wood",r:["trees","conifers","deforestation","paper","architecture","redwoods"],
p:[["A natural material","Wood comes from the trunks and branches of trees."],["Hardwood and softwood","Two main kinds with different uses."],["Building with wood","Timber skyscrapers are now being built."]],
l:"Wood is the hard, fibrous material that makes up the trunks and branches of trees and shrubs. It is made mainly of cellulose fibers held together by a natural glue called lignin, and it carries water up from the roots and supports the tree. Each year, a tree adds a new layer of wood under its bark, forming growth rings that show its age. Timber, or lumber, is wood that has been cut and prepared for building and making things. Wood is usually divided into hardwood, from broad-leaved trees such as oak, maple, teak and mahogany, and softwood, from conifers such as pine, spruce and fir, though some ‘softwoods’ are harder than some ‘hardwoods’. People have used wood for fuel, tools, shelter, boats, furniture and paper for hundreds of thousands of years, and it remains one of the most widely used materials in the world. Wood from well-managed forests is renewable and stores carbon. Engineered woods such as cross-laminated timber are now strong enough to build tall buildings, but illegal logging and deforestation remain serious problems.",
s:[["Inside wood","The outer, living part of a trunk is the sapwood, which carries water. The darker inner heartwood is dead but strong and often resistant to rot. Lignin, which stiffens the cell walls, is what makes wood hard."],["Using wood","About half of the wood cut worldwide is burned for cooking and heating, mainly in developing countries. The rest is used for building, furniture, paper, packaging and many other products."],["Tall timber","Cross-laminated timber is made by gluing layers of wood at right angles, creating strong panels. Timber buildings over 80 meters tall have been completed, such as Ascent in Milwaukee, United States, finished in 2022."],["Sustainable forestry","Certification schemes such as the Forest Stewardship Council label wood from responsibly managed forests. Illegal logging, especially of tropical hardwoods, harms forests and communities."]],
k:[["Made of","Cellulose fibers and lignin"],["Hardwood","From broad-leaved trees"],["Softwood","From conifers"],["Growth rings","One per year"],["Engineered wood","Cross-laminated timber, plywood"],["Main use worldwide","Fuel for cooking and heating"]],
d:[["c. 400,000 years ago","Wooden spears made at Schöningen, Germany"],["1993","Forest Stewardship Council founded"],["2022","Ascent, a tall timber building, completed"]],
s2:[["Floating and sinking","Most woods float because they contain air, but some very dense woods, such as lignum vitae and some ebonies, sink in water."],["Oldest wooden structure","Wooden logs about 476,000 years old, found at Kalambo Falls in Zambia and described in 2023, appear to have been shaped by early humans."]],
g:[["Lignin","A natural substance that makes wood hard"],["Timber","Wood prepared for building"],["Heartwood","The dense inner wood of a trunk"],["Cross-laminated timber","Layers of wood glued at right angles"]],
f:["Wood is made of cellulose and lignin.","Softwood comes from conifers.","Timber buildings can be very tall."],
fun:"Balsa, one of the lightest woods, is technically a hardwood."});

ADDART({id:"saltflats",t:"Salt Flats",c:"Earth Science",i:"🧂",w:"Salt pan (geology)",r:["desert","lakes","boliviacountry","lithium","oases"],
p:[["Ancient lakes","Salt flats form where lakes dried up."],["Flat as a table","Some are among the flattest places on Earth."],["A giant mirror","After rain, they reflect the sky perfectly."]],
l:"A salt flat, or salt pan, is a wide, flat area of ground covered in salt and other minerals, left behind when a lake or pond evaporated. They form in dry regions where water flows into a basin with no outlet to the sea; as the water evaporates in the heat, the dissolved salts it carried are left behind, building up a crust that can be meters thick. The largest salt flat in the world is the Salar de Uyuni in Bolivia, covering about 10,500 square kilometers at about 3,650 meters above sea level. It is so flat that its height varies by less than a meter across its whole surface, and satellites use it to calibrate their instruments. After rain, a thin layer of water turns it into a vast mirror reflecting the sky. Other famous salt flats include the Bonneville Salt Flats in Utah, United States, used for land speed records, Etosha Pan in Namibia, the salt flats of the Danakil Depression in Ethiopia, and Chott el Djerid in Tunisia. The brines beneath some salt flats in South America are rich in lithium, used in batteries.",
s:[["Salar de Uyuni","Uyuni formed from prehistoric lakes that dried up thousands of years ago. Its crust is crossed by hexagonal patterns formed as salt crystallizes. Flamingos breed nearby, and hotels have been built from salt blocks."],["Speed records","The hard, flat surface of the Bonneville Salt Flats has been used for land speed records since the early 1900s. Racers still gather there each August for Speed Week."],["Lithium","The ‘Lithium Triangle’ of Argentina, Bolivia and Chile holds more than half of the world’s known lithium resources in brines under salt flats. Mining it uses lots of water in dry areas, which concerns local communities."],["Salt harvesting","People have harvested salt from salt flats for thousands of years. In the Danakil Depression of Ethiopia, workers still cut slabs of salt by hand and carry them away by camel caravan."]],
k:[["Formed by","Evaporating lakes in dry basins"],["Largest","Salar de Uyuni, Bolivia"],["Uyuni area","About 10,500 km²"],["Uyuni altitude","About 3,650 m"],["Speed records","Bonneville Salt Flats, Utah"],["Hidden resource","Lithium-rich brines"]],
s2:[["Mirror effect","When covered with a few centimeters of water, Uyuni becomes one of the largest natural mirrors on Earth, popular with photographers."],["Salt on Mars","Spacecraft have found salt deposits on Mars, suggesting ancient lakes there dried up too."]],
g:[["Salt flat","A flat area covered in salt from a dried lake"],["Evaporation","When liquid turns to gas"],["Brine","Very salty water"],["Basin","A low area where water collects"]],
f:["Salt flats form when lakes evaporate.","Salar de Uyuni is the largest salt flat.","Some salt flats hold lithium."],
fun:"Salar de Uyuni is so flat that satellites use it to check that their height measurements are accurate."});

ADDART({id:"midoceanridges",t:"Mid-Ocean Ridges",c:"Earth Science",i:"🌋",w:"Mid-ocean ridge",r:["tect","volc","trench","iceland","quake"],
p:[["Mountains under the sea","The longest mountain chain on Earth is underwater."],["New seafloor","Plates pull apart and magma rises to fill the gap."],["Hot springs","Vents gush hot, mineral-rich water."]],
l:"A mid-ocean ridge is a long underwater mountain range where two of Earth’s tectonic plates are moving apart. As the plates separate, hot rock from the mantle below rises, partly melts and erupts as lava, cooling to form new ocean floor. This process is called seafloor spreading. The world’s mid-ocean ridges join into a system about 65,000 kilometers long, the longest mountain chain on the planet, winding through every ocean like the seams on a baseball. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge runs down the middle of the Atlantic Ocean from near the Arctic to near Antarctica, and is widening the ocean by about 2 to 5 centimeters a year, about as fast as fingernails grow. In a few places, such as Iceland, a ridge rises above sea level. Along the ridges are hydrothermal vents, where seawater heated by magma gushes out at temperatures of up to about 400 °C, supporting communities of tubeworms, clams and microbes that live without sunlight. The discovery of seafloor spreading in the 1960s was key evidence for the theory of plate tectonics.",
s:[["Mapping the ridges","In the 1950s, the American geologist Marie Tharp, working with Bruce Heezen, used ocean depth measurements to map the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and found a deep valley along its center, a rift where the plates pull apart."],["Magnetic stripes","As new rock forms, iron minerals line up with Earth’s magnetic field, which flips direction from time to time. This leaves matching stripes on each side of a ridge, like a tape recording, which proved seafloor spreading in the 1960s."],["Fast and slow","The East Pacific Rise spreads up to about 15 centimeters a year and has a smooth shape. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge spreads slowly and has a rugged, deep central valley."],["Life at vents","Hydrothermal vents were discovered in 1977 near the Galápagos Islands by scientists in the submersible Alvin. The animals there rely on bacteria that make food from chemicals, a process called chemosynthesis."]],
k:[["Total length","About 65,000 km"],["Formed by","Plates moving apart"],["Process","Seafloor spreading"],["Atlantic widening","About 2–5 cm a year"],["Above sea level","Iceland"],["Vents discovered","1977, near the Galápagos"]],
d:[["1872–76","HMS Challenger expedition finds signs of the Atlantic ridge"],["1957","Tharp and Heezen publish their map"],["1963","Magnetic stripes explained"],["1977","Hydrothermal vents discovered"]],
s2:[["Walk between plates","At Þingvellir in Iceland, visitors can walk through a rift valley between the North American and Eurasian plates, and divers can swim in a crack between them."],["Black smokers","Some vents, called black smokers, release dark clouds of mineral particles that build chimneys several stories tall."]],
g:[["Mid-ocean ridge","An underwater mountain range where plates separate"],["Seafloor spreading","The creation of new ocean floor at ridges"],["Rift","A crack where Earth’s crust pulls apart"],["Hydrothermal vent","An opening releasing heated water"]],
f:["Mid-ocean ridges are where plates move apart.","The Atlantic Ocean is getting wider.","Iceland sits on a mid-ocean ridge."],
fun:"The Atlantic Ocean widens each year by about as much as your fingernails grow."});

ADDART({id:"oases",t:"Oases",c:"Earth Science",i:"🌴",w:"Oasis",r:["desert","sahara","palms","silk","wcycle","saltflats"],
p:[["Water in the desert","An oasis is a fertile spot fed by underground water."],["Date palms","Oases often grow dates, fruits and vegetables."],["Trade routes","Caravans traveled from oasis to oasis."]],
l:"An oasis is a fertile area in a desert where water reaches the surface or lies close enough to dig for, allowing plants to grow and people and animals to live. Most oases are fed by groundwater stored in underground layers of rock called aquifers, which may have been filled by rain thousands of years ago or by water flowing underground from distant mountains. The water may rise in natural springs or be reached through wells. Oases range from a small pool with a few palm trees to large areas covering hundreds of square kilometers with towns and farms. Date palms are the classic oasis crop: their tall canopy shades smaller fruit trees such as figs, pomegranates and citrus, which in turn shade vegetables and grains below, a system of layered farming. For thousands of years, oases were vital stopping points on trade routes across the Sahara, Arabia and Central Asia, such as the Silk Road. Famous oases include Siwa in Egypt, Al-Ahsa in Saudi Arabia, the largest oasis in the world, and Huacachina in Peru. Many oases are threatened by overuse of groundwater and spreading sand.",
s:[["Al-Ahsa","Al-Ahsa in eastern Saudi Arabia has more than 2.5 million date palms fed by springs and wells, making it one of the largest oases in the world. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Underground channels","In Iran, North Africa and Central Asia, people built underground tunnels called qanats or foggaras that carry groundwater by gravity from hills to oases, some running many kilometers. The qanats of Iran are a World Heritage Site."],["Siwa Oasis","Siwa, in Egypt’s Western Desert, has springs and salt lakes and was home to a famous oracle of the god Amun that Alexander the Great visited in 331 BCE."],["Threats","Pumping groundwater faster than it refills can dry up springs. Sand dunes can bury oasis farms, and young people often move to cities. Some communities are reviving traditional water systems."]],
k:[["Definition","A fertile area in a desert with water"],["Water source","Groundwater from aquifers"],["Classic crop","Date palms"],["Largest","Al-Ahsa, Saudi Arabia"],["Ancient water tunnels","Qanats"],["Threats","Overpumping, sand, depopulation"]],
s2:[["Mirages","Desert travelers sometimes mistake a mirage, light bent by hot air that looks like shimmering water, for an oasis."],["Oases in cities","Several desert cities, such as Damascus and Marrakesh, grew up around oases."]],
g:[["Oasis","A fertile spot in a desert"],["Aquifer","Underground rock that holds water"],["Qanat","An underground channel carrying water"],["Caravan","A group traveling together, often with camels"]],
f:["Oases are fed by groundwater.","Date palms grow in oases.","Al-Ahsa is one of the largest oases."],
fun:"Al-Ahsa has more than 2.5 million date palms."});

ADDART({id:"icebergs",t:"Icebergs",c:"Earth Science",i:"🧊",w:"Iceberg",r:["glaciers","antarc","arctic","titanic","sealevel","oceancurrents","buoyancy"],
p:[["Floating ice","Huge pieces of ice that break off glaciers."],["Mostly hidden","About 90 percent of an iceberg is underwater."],["Giant bergs","Some are larger than small countries."]],
l:"An iceberg is a large piece of freshwater ice that has broken off a glacier or ice shelf and floats in the sea. The breaking off is called calving. Icebergs are made of compressed snow that fell many years earlier, so they are fresh, not salty, and can be thousands of years old. Because ice is slightly less dense than seawater, an iceberg floats with only about a tenth of its volume above the surface; the rest is hidden below, which is where the saying ‘the tip of the iceberg’ comes from. Most icebergs come from Antarctica and Greenland. Those from Antarctica’s ice shelves are often flat-topped and enormous: in 2000 an iceberg called B-15, about 11,000 square kilometers, broke from the Ross Ice Shelf, the largest ever recorded. Greenland’s glaciers produce many smaller, jagged icebergs, some of which drift south into the North Atlantic shipping lanes; one sank the Titanic in 1912, leading to the creation of the International Ice Patrol. As icebergs melt, they release nutrients that feed plankton, and their freshwater adds to the ocean.",
s:[["Shapes and colors","Icebergs can be tabular (flat-topped), domed, pinnacled or wedge-shaped. Old, dense ice can look deep blue because it absorbs red light. Some icebergs are striped with mud or green from frozen algae."],["Giant icebergs","Iceberg A23a, which broke from Antarctica in 1986, stayed grounded for decades before drifting north in the 2020s and slowly breaking apart. Scientists track large icebergs by satellite and give them letters and numbers."],["Ice Patrol","After the Titanic disaster, countries set up the International Ice Patrol in 1914 to watch for icebergs in the North Atlantic and warn ships. It now uses aircraft and satellites."],["Melting and sea level","Icebergs already floating do not raise sea level when they melt, but ice flowing from land into the sea does. Faster calving from Greenland and Antarctica is adding to sea level rise."]],
k:[["Made of","Fresh water (compressed snow)"],["Below the surface","About 90%"],["Formed by","Calving from glaciers and ice shelves"],["Main sources","Antarctica and Greenland"],["Largest recorded","B-15, about 11,000 km² (2000)"],["Ice Patrol founded","1914"]],
d:[["1912","Titanic sinks after hitting an iceberg"],["1914","International Ice Patrol founded"],["1986","Iceberg A23a breaks off Antarctica"],["2000","Iceberg B-15 calves"]],
s2:[["Growlers and bergy bits","Small pieces of floating ice have names too: bergy bits are about the size of a house, and growlers about the size of a car."],["Iceberg Alley","Off the coast of Newfoundland and Labrador in Canada, ‘Iceberg Alley’ is a popular place to watch icebergs drift past in spring."]],
g:[["Iceberg","A large floating piece of glacier ice"],["Calving","Ice breaking off a glacier"],["Ice shelf","A floating sheet of ice attached to land"],["Tabular iceberg","A flat-topped iceberg"]],
f:["Icebergs are made of fresh water.","Most of an iceberg is underwater.","An iceberg sank the Titanic."],
fun:"When icebergs melt, they fizz, as tiny bubbles of ancient air trapped in the ice escape."});

ADDART({id:"pancreas",t:"The Pancreas",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🫁",w:"Pancreas",r:["insulin","digest","diabetes","hormone","liver","metabolism"],
p:[["Two jobs in one","Makes digestive juices and hormones."],["Insulin","It controls blood sugar."],["Hidden organ","It lies behind the stomach."]],
l:"The pancreas is an organ about 15 centimeters long that lies deep in the abdomen, behind the stomach. It has two very different jobs. First, it is a digestive gland: it makes about 1 to 1.5 liters of pancreatic juice a day, full of enzymes that break down proteins, fats and carbohydrates, and sends it through a duct into the small intestine. The juice also contains bicarbonate, which neutralizes the acid coming from the stomach. Second, it is a hormone gland. Scattered through the pancreas are about a million tiny clusters of cells called islets of Langerhans, which release hormones directly into the blood. The most important are insulin, which lowers blood sugar by helping cells take in glucose, and glucagon, which raises blood sugar when it is low. When the islets do not make enough insulin, or the body does not respond to it properly, a person has diabetes. Other conditions affecting the pancreas include pancreatitis, an inflammation often caused by gallstones or heavy drinking, and pancreatic cancer.",
s:[["Islets of Langerhans","The islets were discovered in 1869 by the German medical student Paul Langerhans, who did not know their purpose. They make up only about 1 to 2 percent of the pancreas but are vital for controlling blood sugar."],["Discovering insulin","In 1921–22, Frederick Banting, Charles Best, James Collip and John Macleod in Toronto, Canada, extracted insulin and used it to treat a 14-year-old boy, Leonard Thompson, who had type 1 diabetes. Banting and Macleod won the 1923 Nobel Prize."],["Type 1 and type 2 diabetes","In type 1 diabetes, the immune system destroys the insulin-making cells. In type 2, the body does not respond well to insulin and may not make enough. Both lead to high blood sugar if not treated."],["Transplants","Pancreas and islet transplants can restore insulin production for some people with severe type 1 diabetes. Researchers are also testing islets grown from stem cells."]],
k:[["Location","Behind the stomach"],["Length","About 15 cm"],["Digestive juice","About 1–1.5 liters a day"],["Key hormones","Insulin and glucagon"],["Hormone cells","Islets of Langerhans"],["Related disease","Diabetes"]],
d:[["1869","Paul Langerhans describes the islets"],["1889","Removing the pancreas shown to cause diabetes in dogs"],["1922","Insulin first used to treat diabetes"],["1923","Nobel Prize for insulin"]],
s2:[["Sweetbreads","In cooking, the pancreas of calves or lambs is sometimes eaten as ‘sweetbread’."],["Blood sugar balance","Insulin and glucagon work like a seesaw, keeping blood sugar within a narrow healthy range."]],
g:[["Pancreas","An organ making digestive juices and hormones"],["Insulin","A hormone that lowers blood sugar"],["Glucagon","A hormone that raises blood sugar"],["Pancreatitis","Inflammation of the pancreas"]],
f:["The pancreas makes insulin.","The pancreas makes digestive enzymes.","Insulin was first used in 1922."],
fun:"The first person treated with insulin, in 1922, was a 14-year-old boy in Toronto."});

ADDART({id:"cholesterol",t:"Cholesterol",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🩸",w:"Cholesterol",r:["heartdisease","circulation","nutri","liver","hypertension","exercise"],
p:[["Needed by the body","Cholesterol builds cells and hormones."],["Good and bad","HDL and LDL carry it in the blood."],["Too much","High LDL can clog arteries."]],
l:"Cholesterol is a waxy, fat-like substance found in every cell of the body. It is essential: the body uses it to build cell membranes, to make vitamin D and hormones such as estrogen, testosterone and cortisol, and to make bile, which helps digest fats. The liver makes most of the cholesterol the body needs, and some comes from foods of animal origin, such as meat, eggs and dairy. Because cholesterol does not dissolve in blood, it is carried by particles called lipoproteins. Low-density lipoprotein (LDL), often called ‘bad’ cholesterol, carries cholesterol to the tissues; too much LDL can build up in the walls of arteries, forming fatty plaques that narrow them, a process called atherosclerosis, which raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes. High-density lipoprotein (HDL), sometimes called ‘good’ cholesterol, carries extra cholesterol back to the liver. Cholesterol levels are affected by genes, diet, weight, exercise and smoking. High cholesterol usually causes no symptoms, so it is found with a blood test, and it can be lowered with lifestyle changes and medicines such as statins.",
s:[["LDL and HDL","Think of LDL as delivery trucks dropping cholesterol off and HDL as trucks collecting it. High LDL is linked to heart disease. Higher HDL has usually been linked to lower risk, though raising HDL with drugs has not proved helpful."],["Diet","Eating less saturated fat, found in fatty meats, butter and some oils, and trans fats can lower LDL. Fiber from oats, beans and fruit also helps. For most people, cholesterol in food has a smaller effect than saturated fat."],["Statins","Statins, first approved in 1987, block an enzyme the liver uses to make cholesterol. Large studies show they lower the risk of heart attacks and strokes in people at high risk. Other drugs can lower LDL further when needed."],["Inherited high cholesterol","Familial hypercholesterolemia is an inherited condition causing very high LDL from birth. It affects roughly 1 in 250 people, and early treatment can prevent heart disease."]],
k:[["Made by","Mainly the liver"],["Used for","Cell membranes, hormones, vitamin D, bile"],["‘Bad’ cholesterol","LDL"],["‘Good’ cholesterol","HDL"],["Risk","Atherosclerosis, heart attack, stroke"],["Common medicine","Statins"]],
d:[["1769","Cholesterol first found in gallstones"],["1913","Nikolai Anichkov links cholesterol to artery disease"],["1985","Nobel Prize for research on how cells take in LDL"],["1987","First statin approved"]],
s2:[["Plants","Plants do not make cholesterol, but they contain similar substances called plant sterols, which can help block cholesterol absorption."],["Brain","About a quarter of the body’s cholesterol is in the brain, where it helps insulate nerve fibers."]],
g:[["Cholesterol","A waxy substance in cells"],["Lipoprotein","A particle carrying fats in the blood"],["Atherosclerosis","Hardening and narrowing of arteries"],["Statin","A medicine that lowers cholesterol"]],
f:["The liver makes most cholesterol.","LDL can build up in arteries.","High cholesterol usually has no symptoms."],
fun:"About a quarter of all the cholesterol in your body is in your brain."});

ADDART({id:"handedness",t:"Left- and Right-Handedness",c:"Health and the Body",i:"✋",w:"Handedness",r:["brain","nervous","genome","twins","writing"],
p:[["Most people are right-handed","About 9 in 10 people prefer their right hand."],["Lefties","About 10 percent are left-handed."],["Genes and chance","Handedness comes from many genes and early development."]],
l:"Handedness is a person’s natural preference for using one hand over the other for tasks such as writing, throwing and using tools. Across the world, about 90 percent of people are right-handed and about 10 percent are left-handed, with a small number ambidextrous, able to use both hands about equally well. This balance appears to have been similar for thousands of years, judging from ancient tools, cave art handprints and teeth marks. Handedness is linked to how the brain is organized: each side of the brain controls the opposite side of the body, and in most people, language is handled mainly by the left half. Scientists believe handedness is influenced by many genes, each with a tiny effect, together with chance events during development before birth; even identical twins can have different handedness. Babies may show a preference in the womb, and most children settle on a dominant hand by about age 4 to 6. In the past, many left-handed children were forced to write with their right hands, a practice now discouraged in most countries.",
s:[["The brain","About 95 percent of right-handers process language mainly in the left side of the brain. For left-handers the figure is around 70 percent; the rest use the right side or both."],["Left-handers in sport","Left-handers are more common at the top of interactive sports such as tennis, fencing, cricket, baseball and boxing, perhaps because opponents are less used to facing them."],["Tools and design","Many everyday objects, such as scissors, can openers and some desks, are designed for right-handers. Left-handed versions are now widely available."],["Words and beliefs","In many languages, words for ‘right’ also mean correct or skillful, while ‘left’ words had negative meanings; the English word ‘sinister’ comes from the Latin for ‘left’. Such beliefs led to left-handers being stigmatized in the past."]],
k:[["Right-handed","About 90% of people"],["Left-handed","About 10%"],["Language side (right-handers)","Usually the left brain"],["Decided by","Many genes plus chance"],["Hand preference settles","By about age 4–6"],["International Left-Handers Day","13 August"]],
s2:[["Other sides","People also have a preferred foot, eye and ear, which do not always match their dominant hand."],["Animals","Some animals also show handedness, such as parrots favoring one foot for holding food and kangaroos favoring their left paw."]],
g:[["Handedness","Preference for one hand"],["Ambidextrous","Able to use both hands equally well"],["Dominant hand","The hand a person prefers"],["Hemisphere","One half of the brain"]],
f:["About 10 percent of people are left-handed.","Each side of the brain controls the opposite side of the body.","Handedness is influenced by many genes."],
fun:"The word ‘sinister’ comes from the Latin word for ‘left’."});
